1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,615
Hold your breath.

2
00:00:01,615 --> 00:00:04,565
After a while, your body
forces you to breathe.

3
00:00:04,565 --> 00:00:08,488
Sprint on a bicycle, and you breathe
faster without even thinking.

4
00:00:08,888 --> 00:00:12,357
Something is watching your blood
and adjusting every breath.

5
00:00:12,807 --> 00:00:15,972
Aarav tried to hold his breath
for a whole minute after class.

6
00:00:15,972 --> 00:00:18,250
He asked: who decides when I must breathe?

7
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,567
And what goes wrong when
breathing gets hard?

8
00:00:20,917 --> 00:00:22,660
Here is the short answer.

9
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:27,344
Centres in your brain set the rhythm,
and they react mostly to carbon dioxide

10
00:00:27,344 --> 00:00:28,631
and hydrogen ions.

11
00:00:28,631 --> 00:00:32,802
Disorders like asthma and emphysema
harm the airways or the alveoli.

12
00:00:33,302 --> 00:00:37,533
By the end, you will know the respiratory
rhythm centre, the pneumotaxic

13
00:00:37,533 --> 00:00:41,264
centre, the chemical sensors,
and three respiratory disorders.

14
00:00:41,914 --> 00:00:44,210
First, breathing runs on autopilot.

15
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,707
You can change your breathing for a
while, like holding it or breathing

16
00:00:48,707 --> 00:00:49,607
deeper.

17
00:00:49,318 --> 00:00:53,660
But most of the time, your nervous
system controls it, to match what the

18
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:54,560
body needs.

19
00:00:54,962 --> 00:00:57,238
The control room is in the brain.

20
00:00:57,238 --> 00:01:01,566
A special centre in the medulla,
called the respiratory rhythm centre,

21
00:01:01,566 --> 00:01:03,627
is mainly responsible for this.

22
00:01:04,277 --> 00:01:07,317
Let's zoom into the brain
to find the control room.

23
00:01:07,667 --> 00:01:09,033
Here is the brain.

24
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:12,874
At its base, joining the
spinal cord, is the brain stem.

25
00:01:13,374 --> 00:01:15,116
Zoom into the brain stem.

26
00:01:15,116 --> 00:01:17,014
The upper part is the pons.

27
00:01:17,014 --> 00:01:19,057
The lower part is the medulla.

28
00:01:19,557 --> 00:01:20,457
Zoom closer.

29
00:01:20,349 --> 00:01:24,034
In the medulla sits the respiratory
rhythm centre, with the chemosensitive

30
00:01:24,034 --> 00:01:25,382
area right next to it.

31
00:01:25,382 --> 00:01:27,564
In the pons sits the pneumotaxic centre.

32
00:01:28,214 --> 00:01:33,190
Now follow what happens when carbon
dioxide builds up, step by step.

33
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:34,440
Step one.

34
00:01:34,323 --> 00:01:37,748
You exercise, and your cells
make more carbon dioxide.

35
00:01:37,748 --> 00:01:40,209
Hydrogen ions in the blood go up too.

36
00:01:40,959 --> 00:01:41,859
Step two.

37
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:45,917
The chemosensitive area, next to
the rhythm centre, senses this rise.

38
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:49,419
It is highly sensitive to carbon
dioxide and hydrogen ions.

39
00:01:50,169 --> 00:01:51,069
Step three.

40
00:01:50,968 --> 00:01:54,759
Receptors on the aortic arch and the
carotid artery, big blood vessels

41
00:01:54,759 --> 00:01:57,536
near the heart and in
the neck, sense it too.

42
00:01:58,286 --> 00:01:59,186
Step four.

43
00:01:59,052 --> 00:02:02,171
They all send signals to the
respiratory rhythm centre.

44
00:02:02,921 --> 00:02:03,821
Step five.

45
00:02:03,717 --> 00:02:07,854
The rhythm centre adjusts your breathing,
so the extra carbon dioxide and

46
00:02:07,854 --> 00:02:09,492
hydrogen ions are removed.

47
00:02:10,242 --> 00:02:11,346
Remember this.

48
00:02:11,346 --> 00:02:14,822
Carbon dioxide, not oxygen,
drives your breathing.

49
00:02:15,422 --> 00:02:18,482
Now see the centres and
sensors as one system.

50
00:02:18,832 --> 00:02:22,664
The respiratory rhythm centre in
the medulla sets the basic rhythm.

51
00:02:22,664 --> 00:02:25,669
The pneumotaxic centre in
the pons can moderate it.

52
00:02:25,669 --> 00:02:29,924
Its signal can shorten each breath
in, and so change the breathing rate.

53
00:02:30,374 --> 00:02:31,288
And oxygen?

54
00:02:31,288 --> 00:02:34,756
Its role in setting the
rhythm is quite insignificant.

55
00:02:34,756 --> 00:02:38,015
The sensors watch carbon
dioxide and hydrogen ions.

56
00:02:38,665 --> 00:02:40,677
Now, when breathing goes wrong.

57
00:02:40,677 --> 00:02:42,077
Compare two disorders.

58
00:02:42,427 --> 00:02:46,226
Asthma is difficulty in breathing
with wheezing, a whistling sound.

59
00:02:46,226 --> 00:02:50,530
It is caused by inflammation, or
swelling, of the bronchi and bronchioles.

60
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:55,440
Emphysema is a long-lasting disorder
in which the walls of the alveoli

61
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:56,465
are damaged.

62
00:02:56,465 --> 00:02:59,392
The surface for gas exchange gets smaller.

63
00:02:59,392 --> 00:03:01,886
A major cause is cigarette smoking.

64
00:03:02,536 --> 00:03:04,081
There is a third kind.

65
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:06,301
Some jobs can make the lungs sick.

66
00:03:06,651 --> 00:03:10,005
In industries like grinding and
stone-breaking, so much dust is made that

67
00:03:10,005 --> 00:03:11,581
the body's defences cannot cope.

68
00:03:12,031 --> 00:03:17,012
Long exposure causes inflammation, then
fibrosis, which means extra fibrous

69
00:03:17,012 --> 00:03:18,117
tissue grows.

70
00:03:18,117 --> 00:03:20,618
This leads to serious lung damage.

71
00:03:20,618 --> 00:03:23,216
Workers should wear protective masks.

72
00:03:23,816 --> 00:03:24,716
Your turn.

73
00:03:24,681 --> 00:03:28,815
In emphysema, does the surface
for gas exchange grow or shrink?

74
00:03:28,815 --> 00:03:30,087
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:33,287 --> 00:03:34,187
It shrinks!

76
00:03:34,171 --> 00:03:38,619
The alveolar walls are damaged, so
less surface is left to swap gases.

77
00:03:39,269 --> 00:03:40,870
Now a worked example.

78
00:03:40,870 --> 00:03:42,835
Three people, three clues.

79
00:03:42,835 --> 00:03:44,218
Name the disorder.

80
00:03:44,568 --> 00:03:48,185
Person one wheezes, and the bronchi
and bronchioles are swollen.

81
00:03:48,185 --> 00:03:49,214
That is asthma.

82
00:03:49,664 --> 00:03:54,535
Person two smoked for many years,
and the alveolar walls are damaged.

83
00:03:54,535 --> 00:03:55,969
That is emphysema.

84
00:03:56,419 --> 00:04:01,801
Person three cut stone for years without
a mask, and the lungs show fibrosis.

85
00:04:01,801 --> 00:04:04,856
That is an occupational
respiratory disorder.

86
00:04:05,506 --> 00:04:06,970
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,713
Many students place the
pneumotaxic centre in the medulla.

88
00:04:10,113 --> 00:04:11,507
But it is in the pons.

89
00:04:11,507 --> 00:04:15,363
The rhythm centre and the chemosensitive
area are the ones in the medulla.

90
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:17,380
Where is this asked?

91
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:21,576
As statement or match questions on the
brain centres, the chemical sensors

92
00:04:21,576 --> 00:04:23,127
and the three disorders.

93
00:04:23,727 --> 00:04:24,859
Exam level now.

94
00:04:24,859 --> 00:04:27,074
Read the assertion and the reason.

95
00:04:27,074 --> 00:04:28,254
Pause and solve.

96
00:04:33,654 --> 00:04:34,921
The answer is B.

97
00:04:34,921 --> 00:04:39,508
Both statements are true, but the
reason does not explain the assertion.

98
00:04:40,008 --> 00:04:43,715
Breathing speeds up because the
chemosensitive area and the aortic and

99
00:04:43,715 --> 00:04:46,462
carotid receptors sense
the extra carbon dioxide.

100
00:04:46,962 --> 00:04:51,453
The pneumotaxic centre is indeed in the
pons, but that fact does not explain

101
00:04:51,453 --> 00:04:53,422
the response to carbon dioxide.

102
00:04:54,022 --> 00:04:55,563
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:55,563 --> 00:04:57,147
Screenshot this card.

104
00:05:00,347 --> 00:05:02,533
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:03,833
One.

106
00:05:03,393 --> 00:05:05,700
Where is the respiratory rhythm centre?

107
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:07,500
Two.

108
00:05:06,930 --> 00:05:08,486
What does the pneumotaxic centre do?

109
00:05:09,386 --> 00:05:10,286
Three.

110
00:05:09,923 --> 00:05:11,685
What is damaged in emphysema?

111
00:05:12,785 --> 00:05:17,243
Next time: what is blood really made
of, and how does your heart push it

112
00:05:17,243 --> 00:05:18,397
around the body?

113
00:05:18,397 --> 00:05:21,100
We find out in body
fluids and circulation.
